(* Content-type: application/vnd.wolfram.mathematica *)
(* Ollivier Curvature - Computational Classnotes - CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 *)

Notebook[{
Cell["Ollivier Curvature", "Title"],
Cell["Comparing two averages: coarse Ricci curvature on graphs and on manifolds \:2014 Curvature from the Laplacian, notebook 7", "Subtitle"],
Cell["\:00a9 2026 Computational Classnotes \:00b7 CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "Text"],
Cell["This notebook defines Ollivier\:2019s curvature, works it out by hand on small graphs, computes it on a family of graphs, follows it through laziness to the Lin\:2013Lu\:2013Yau curvature, proves its main consequences on examples, and returns to smooth surfaces, where it recovers the Gauss curvature as \:03ba \:2248 K\:03b5\:00b2/8. Evaluate top to bottom; every section ends with a check. The write-up \:201cOllivier Curvature\:201d carries the same section numbers.", "Text"],
Cell["1. Toolkit", "Section"],
Cell["Graph functions used throughout. \:03bc[G, x, \:03b1] is the lazy random walk at x: mass \:03b1 stays at x and 1 \:2212 \:03b1 is spread evenly over the neighbours. W1 is the Wasserstein-1 (earth mover\:2019s) distance for the graph metric, solved as a linear program; plan returns the optimal transport plan. \:03ba[G, {x, y}, \:03b1] = 1 \:2212 W1(\:03bcx, \:03bcy)/d(x, y) is Ollivier\:2019s curvature for any pair of vertices.", "Text"],
Cell["ClearAll[nbr, \:03bc, W1, plan, W1dual, \:03ba, \:03baLLY, \:03baColour, \:03baLegend];\nnbr[G_, v_] := AdjacencyList[G, v];\n\:03bc[G_, x_, \:03b1_] := Merge[{<|x -> \:03b1|>, AssociationThread[nbr[G, x] -> (1 - \:03b1)/Length[nbr[G, x]]]}, Total];\nplan[G_, m_, n_] := Module[{A = Keys[m], B = Keys[n], p, vars, sol},\n  vars = Flatten@Table[p[a, b], {a, A}, {b, B}];\n  sol = LinearOptimization[Sum[GraphDistance[G, a, b] p[a, b], {a, A}, {b, B}],\n    Join[Table[Sum[p[a, b], {b, B}] == m[a], {a, A}], Table[Sum[p[a, b], {a, A}] == n[b], {b, B}], Thread[vars >= 0]], vars];\n  Select[Association@Flatten@Table[{a, b} -> (p[a, b] /. sol), {a, A}, {b, B}], # > 0 &]];\nW1[G_, m_, n_] := Total@KeyValueMap[GraphDistance[G, #1[[1]], #1[[2]]] #2 &, plan[G, m, n]];\nW1dual[G_, m_, n_] := Module[{S = Union[Keys[m], Keys[n]], f},\n  -LinearOptimization[-(Sum[m[a] f[a], {a, Keys[m]}] - Sum[n[b] f[b], {b, Keys[n]}]),\n    Flatten@Table[f[S[[i]]] - f[S[[j]]] <= GraphDistance[G, S[[i]], S[[j]]], {i, Length[S]}, {j, Length[S]}], f /@ S, \"PrimalMinimumValue\"]];\n\:03ba[G_, {x_, y_}, \:03b1_] := 1 - W1[G, \:03bc[G, x, \:03b1], \:03bc[G, y, \:03b1]]/GraphDistance[G, x, y];\n\:03baLLY[G_, {x_, y_}] := Module[{S, vars, f, cons}, S = Union[{x, y}, nbr[G, x], nbr[G, y]];\n  vars = Table[Unique[\"f\"], {Length[S]}]; f = AssociationThread[S -> vars];\n  cons = Flatten[{f[y] - f[x] == 1, f[x] == 0, Table[With[{d = GraphDistance[G, S[[i]], S[[j]]]},\n      {f[S[[i]]] - f[S[[j]]] <= d, f[S[[j]]] - f[S[[i]]] <= d}], {i, Length[S]}, {j, i + 1, Length[S]}]}];\n  LinearOptimization[(Mean[f /@ nbr[G, x]] - f[x]) - (Mean[f /@ nbr[G, y]] - f[y]), cons, vars, \"PrimalMinimumValue\"]];\n\:03baColour[k_, s_] := Blend[{RGBColor[0.18, 0.36, 0.78], GrayLevel[0.96], RGBColor[0.98, 0.56, 0.17]}, (1 + Clip[k/s, {-1, 1}])/2];\n\:03baLegend[s_, name_] := BarLegend[{\:03baColour[#, s] &, {-s, s}}, LegendLabel -> name];\ntree = KaryTree[15, 2];\ndumbbell = Graph[UndirectedEdge @@@ Join[List @@@ EdgeList[CompleteGraph[5]], (List @@@ EdgeList[CompleteGraph[5]]) + 7, {{5, 6}, {6, 7}, {7, 8}}]];\n{\:03bc[CycleGraph[6], 1, 0], \:03bc[CycleGraph[6], 1, 1/2]}", "Input"],
Cell["Check: the walk on the 6-cycle puts \:00bd on each neighbour and 0 at home; the \:00bd-lazy walk keeps \:00bd at home and \:00bc on each neighbour.", "Text"],
Cell["2. The idea: positive curvature brings averages together", "Section"],
Cell["On a round sphere, two geodesics that leave nearby points in parallel bend towards each other. So a small ball around x, slid along the geodesic to a nearby point y, lands on the ball around y with every point travelling less than the distance d(x, y) between the centres. On a flat plane every point travels exactly d(x, y); on a saddle-shaped surface points off the geodesic travel more. Ollivier (2009) turned this into a definition that needs only distances and averaging: curvature is how much closer the two averaging measures are than their centres.", "Text"],
Cell["Fig. 1: on the unit sphere, points of the \:03b5-ball around x (left) are carried by a rotation onto the \:03b5-ball around y. A point at distance \:03bb from the equator moves along a circle of radius cos \:03bb, so it travels \:03b4 cos \:03bb < \:03b4. Arrow colour shows the ratio travelled/\:03b4 = cos \:03bb: orange 1 (on the geodesic), blue the smallest.", "Text"],
Cell["With[{\:03b4 = 0.7, \:03b5 = 0.42, R = RotationTransform[0.7, {0, 0, 1}]},\n Module[{ball, pts, arrows},\n  ball = Select[SpherePoints[2600], VectorAngle[#, {1, 0, 0}] < \:03b5 &];\n  pts = ball[[;; ;; 7]];\n  arrows = {Blend[{RGBColor[0.18, 0.36, 0.78], RGBColor[0.98, 0.56, 0.17]}, (Sqrt[1 - #[[3]]^2] - Cos[\:03b5])/(1 - Cos[\:03b5])], Arrowheads[0.012], Arrow[Table[1.01 RotationTransform[t, {0, 0, 1}][#], {t, 0, \:03b4, \:03b4/12}]]} & /@ pts;\n  Graphics3D[{{Opacity[0.25], GrayLevel[0.85], Sphere[]},\n    {GrayLevel[0.3], Thick, Line[Table[1.005 {Cos[t], Sin[t], 0}, {t, -0.3, \:03b4 + 0.3, 0.02}]]},\n    {GrayLevel[0.2], Thickness[0.004], Line[Table[1.012 {Cos[\:03b5], Sin[\:03b5] Cos[t], Sin[\:03b5] Sin[t]}, {t, 0, 2 \:03c0, \:03c0/60}]],\n      Line[Table[1.012 R[{Cos[\:03b5], Sin[\:03b5] Cos[t], Sin[\:03b5] Sin[t]}], {t, 0, 2 \:03c0, \:03c0/60}]]}, arrows,\n    {Black, PointSize[0.016], Point[{1.02 {1, 0, 0}, 1.02 R[{1, 0, 0}]}]},\n    Text[Style[\"x\", 14, Bold], 1.18 {1, 0, 0}], Text[Style[\"y\", 14, Bold], 1.18 R[{1, 0, 0}]]},\n   ViewPoint -> {3, 1.3, 1.1}, Boxed -> False, ImageSize -> 420, Lighting -> \"Neutral\",\n   PlotLabel -> Style[\"\:03b5-ball at x slid onto the \:03b5-ball at y: every point moves less than \:03b4\", 11]]]]", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Fig. 1. ", FontWeight -> "Bold", FontColor -> RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11]], "The \:03b5-ball at x on the unit sphere slid by a rotation onto the \:03b5-ball at y; arrow colour is the distance travelled divided by \:03b4."}], "Text"],
Cell["What to see: points on the equator (the geodesic) travel the full \:03b4; points nearer the poles of the ball travel less. The average distance travelled is below \:03b4, so \:03ba > 0.", "Text"],
Cell["3. Definition", "Section"],
Cell["Let (X, d) be a metric space with, for each point x, a probability measure m\:2093 (an averaging rule, or one step of a random walk). The Wasserstein-1 distance between two probability measures is the least average distance needed to move one onto the other:", "Text"],
Cell["W\:2081(m, m\:2032) = min over couplings \:03c0 of \:03a3 \:03c0(a, b) d(a, b),   where \:03c0 \:2265 0 has row sums m(a) and column sums m\:2032(b).", "Text"],
Cell["Ollivier\:2019s curvature of the pair x \:2260 y is", "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"\:03ba(x, y) = 1 \:2212 W\:2081(m\:2093, ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["m", "y"]]], ") / d(x, y)."}], "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"On a graph, d is the shortest-path distance and m\:2093 = \:03bc[G, x, \:03b1] is the \:03b1-lazy simple random walk. Write ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "\:03b1"]]], " for the resulting curvature; \:03ba\:2080 is the non-lazy one. Kantorovich\:2013Rubinstein duality gives a second formula, a maximum over 1-Lipschitz test functions:"}], "Text"],
Cell["W\:2081(m, m\:2032) = max over f with |f(a) \:2212 f(b)| \:2264 d(a, b) of  \:03a3 f(a) m(a) \:2212 \:03a3 f(b) m\:2032(b).", "Text"],
Cell["So an explicit transport plan bounds W\:2081 above (\:03ba below), an explicit 1-Lipschitz function bounds W\:2081 below (\:03ba above), and when the two meet the value is proved. The check computes W\:2081 both ways, as the primal and the dual linear program.", "Text"],
Cell["With[{cases = {{CycleGraph[6], {1, 2}}, {CompleteGraph[5], {1, 2}}, {tree, {2, 4}}, {PetersenGraph[], {1, 3}}, {HypercubeGraph[3], {1, 2}}}},\n Table[With[{G = c[[1]], x = c[[2, 1]], y = c[[2, 2]]},\n   {W1[G, \:03bc[G, x, 0], \:03bc[G, y, 0]], W1dual[G, \:03bc[G, x, 0], \:03bc[G, y, 0]]}], {c, cases}]]", "Input"],
Cell["Check: each pair is equal \:2014 {1, 1}, {1/4, 1/4}, {5/3, 5/3}, {4/3, 4/3}, {1, 1} \:2014 so the transport plans found are optimal.", "Text"],
Cell["4. Worked by hand", "Section"],
Cell["4.1 Complete graph K\:2099, non-lazy walk", "Subsection"],
Cell[TextData[{"Take adjacent x, y in K\:2099. The walk at x puts 1/(n \:2212 1) on y and on each of the n \:2212 2 common neighbours; the walk at y puts 1/(n \:2212 1) on x and on the same n \:2212 2 common neighbours. The common masses already match, so leave them. Only the mass 1/(n \:2212 1) sitting on y has to move to x, a distance 1. Hence W\:2081 \:2264 1/(n \:2212 1). The test function f = 1 at y, 0 elsewhere is 1-Lipschitz and gives \:03a3 f m\:2093 \:2212 \:03a3 f ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["m", "y"]]], " = 1/(n \:2212 1), so W\:2081 = 1/(n \:2212 1) exactly and"}], "Text"],
Cell["\:03ba\:2080(x, y) = 1 \:2212 1/(n \:2212 1) = (n \:2212 2)/(n \:2212 1).", "Text"],
Cell["Table[{n, \:03ba[CompleteGraph[n], {1, 2}, 0], (n - 2)/(n - 1)}, {n, 3, 7}]", "Input"],
Cell["4.2 Cycle C\:2099, n \:2265 4", "Subsection"],
Cell[TextData[{"Number the cycle so x = 1, y = 2. Then m\:2093 puts \:00bd on 0 and \:00bd on 2, and ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["m", "y"]]], " puts \:00bd on 1 and \:00bd on 3. Move 0 \:2192 1 and 2 \:2192 3, each a distance 1 carrying \:00bd: W\:2081 \:2264 1. The function f = 1 on 0 and 2, f = 0 elsewhere, is 1-Lipschitz and gives \:00bd + \:00bd \:2212 0 = 1. So W\:2081 = 1 and \:03ba\:2080 = 0: long cycles are flat. For n = 3 the cycle is K\:2083 and \:03ba\:2080 = \:00bd."}], "Text"],
Cell["Table[{n, \:03ba[CycleGraph[n], {1, 2}, 0]}, {n, 3, 8}]", "Input"],
Cell["4.3 An interior edge of the binary tree", "Subsection"],
Cell["In the binary tree with 15 vertices take the edge x = 2, y = 4 (both of degree 3). Each walk puts \:2153 on the other endpoint and \:2153 on each of two further neighbours. The neighbours of x other than y lie at distance 3 from the neighbours of y other than x. One optimal plan moves the mass \:2153 on y to a neighbour of y (distance 1), the mass \:2153 on the far neighbour of x to x (distance 1), and the last \:2153 across, distance 3: W\:2081 = \:2153 + \:2153 + 1 = 5/3, so \:03ba\:2080 = \:22122/3. Trees spread out: negative curvature.", "Text"],
Cell["{\:03ba[tree, {2, 4}, 0], plan[tree, \:03bc[tree, 2, 0], \:03bc[tree, 4, 0]]}", "Input"],
Cell["The plan lists (from, to) \:2192 mass. Fig. 2 shows the optimal plans for one edge of K\:2085, C\:2086 and the tree: the edge in black, mass moved as orange arrows labelled by the amount.", "Text"],
Cell["planPicture[G_, {x_, y_}, label_] := Module[{pl = plan[G, \:03bc[G, x, 0], \:03bc[G, y, 0]], pos, moves},\n  pos = AssociationThread[VertexList[G] -> GraphEmbedding[G]];\n  moves = Select[Normal[pl], #[[1, 1]] =!= #[[1, 2]] &];\n  Show[Graph[G, VertexSize -> 0.12, VertexStyle -> GrayLevel[0.85], EdgeStyle -> GrayLevel[0.8],\n     VertexLabels -> None, ImageSize -> If[VertexCount[G] > 10, 360, 230]],\n   Graphics[{{Black, Thickness[0.012], Line[{pos[x], pos[y]}]},\n     Table[With[{a = m[[1, 1]], b = m[[1, 2]], w = m[[2]]},\n       {RGBColor[0.98, 0.56, 0.17], Thickness[0.006], Arrowheads[0.06],\n        Arrow[{pos[a], pos[b]}, 0.08], Black, Text[Style[w, 10, Background -> White], pos[a] + 0.3 (pos[b] - pos[a])]}], {m, moves}]}],\n   PlotLabel -> Style[Row[{label, \":  W\:2081 = \", W1[G, \:03bc[G, x, 0], \:03bc[G, y, 0]], \",  \:03ba\:2080 = \", \:03ba[G, {x, y}, 0]}], 10], ImagePadding -> {{10, 10}, {10, 34}}]];\nRow[{planPicture[CompleteGraph[5], {1, 2}, \"K\:2085\"], planPicture[CycleGraph[6], {1, 2}, \"C\:2086\"], planPicture[tree, {2, 4}, \"binary tree\"]}, \"  \"]", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Fig. 2. ", FontWeight -> "Bold", FontColor -> RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11]], "Optimal transport plans for one edge of K\:2085, C\:2086 and the binary tree; the edge is black, moved mass is orange."}], "Text"],
Cell["What to see: in K\:2085 one arrow of mass \:00bc; in C\:2086 two arrows of mass \:00bd; in the tree some mass has to cross the edge and travel 3.", "Text"],
Cell["5. Laziness and the Lin\:2013Lu\:2013Yau curvature", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"Bipartite graphs show a defect of the non-lazy walk: on the cube Q\:2083 the walk at x lives on the odd vertices and the walk at y on the even ones, so every unit of mass must move and \:03ba\:2080 = 0, although the lazy walk below shows the cube is positively curved. Laziness repairs this. The idleness function \:03b1 \:21a6 ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "\:03b1"]]], "(x, y) is concave and piecewise linear, and on [1/(d + 1), 1], with d = max(deg x, deg y), it is linear and vanishes at \:03b1 = 1 (Bourne, Cushing, Liu, M\:00fcnch and Peyerimhoff 2018). Lin, Lu and Yau (2011) defined ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "LLY"]]], " = lim ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "\:03b1"]]], "/(1 \:2212 \:03b1) as \:03b1 \:2192 1. Linearity gives, for every \:03b1 in that interval,"}], "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "LLY"]]], "(x, y) = ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "\:03b1"]]], "(x, y)/(1 \:2212 \:03b1),   in particular ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "LLY"]]], " = 2 ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "1/2"]]], "."}], "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"M\:00fcnch and Wojciechowski (2019) showed ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "LLY"]]], "(x, y) = min{ \:2207\:2093\:1d67 \:0394f : f 1-Lipschitz, f(y) \:2212 f(x) = 1 }, a formula in the graph Laplacian \:0394f(v) = mean of f over neighbours \:2212 f(v). This is how \:03baLLY is computed in the toolkit, independently of any transport problem. The check compares the two routes on seven edges."}], "Text"],
Cell["cases = {{\"K\:2085\", CompleteGraph[5], {1, 2}}, {\"C\:2086\", CycleGraph[6], {1, 2}}, {\"Q\:2083\", HypercubeGraph[3], {1, 2}}, {\"Petersen\", PetersenGraph[], {1, 3}},\n   {\"tree\", tree, {2, 4}}, {\"dumbbell bridge\", dumbbell, {5, 6}}, {\"star\", StarGraph[5], {1, 2}}};\nas = {0, 1/8, 1/6, 1/5, 1/4, 1/3, 1/2, 2/3, 3/4, 9/10};\nidle = Table[With[{G = c[[2]], e = c[[3]]}, {c[[1]], Max[VertexDegree[G, #] & /@ e], \:03ba[G, e, #] & /@ as, \:03baLLY[G, e]}], {c, cases}];\nGrid[Prepend[{#[[1]], #[[2]], Union[Select[Transpose[{as, #[[3]]}], Function[p, p[[1]] >= 1/(#[[2]] + 1)]] /. {a_, k_} :> k/(1 - a)], #[[4]]} & /@ idle,\n  {\"edge\", \"d\", Row[{Subscript[\"\:03ba\", \"\:03b1\"], \"/(1 \:2212 \:03b1) for \:03b1 \:2265 1/(d+1)\"}], Row[{Subscript[\"\:03ba\", \"LLY\"], \" (Laplacian LP)\"}]}], Frame -> All, Background -> {None, {LightGray}}]", "Input"],
Cell["Check: in every row the third column is a single value equal to the fourth \:2014 K\:2085 5/4, C\:2086 0, Q\:2083 2/3, Petersen 0, tree \:22122/3, dumbbell bridge \:22123/5, star 1/2.", "Text"],
Cell["The idleness functions themselves (Fig. 3): each is linear from \:03b1 = 1/(d + 1) down to 0 at \:03b1 = 1; the dot marks \:03b1 = 1/(d + 1). K\:2085 peaks at \:03ba = 1 exactly there.", "Text"],
Cell["ListLinePlot[Table[Append[Transpose[{as, t[[3]]}], {1, 0}], {t, idle}], PlotMarkers -> Automatic, PlotLegends -> idle[[All, 1]],\n Epilog -> {Black, PointSize[0.018], Table[Point[{1/(t[[2]] + 1), \:03ba[cases[[i, 2]], cases[[i, 3]], 1/(t[[2]] + 1)]}], {i, Length[idle]}, {t, {idle[[i]]}}]},\n AxesLabel -> {\"\:03b1\", Subscript[\"\:03ba\", \"\:03b1\"]}, PlotLabel -> \"Idleness functions: linear on [1/(d+1), 1]\", ImageSize -> 520, GridLines -> {{1/2}, {0}}]", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Fig. 3. ", FontWeight -> "Bold", FontColor -> RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11]], "Idleness functions \:03b1 \:21a6 ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "\:03b1"]]], " on seven edges; black dots mark \:03b1 = 1/(d + 1)."}], "Text"],
Cell["6. A gallery of graphs", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"Fig. 4 colours every edge by ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "LLY"]]], " (= 2", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "1/2"]]], ") on the CCN scale: blue negative, near-white zero, orange positive. Complete graphs and cubes are positive, cycles and grids are flat in the interior, trees and bridges are negative."}], "Text"],
Cell["edgePlot[G_, label_] := With[{ks = \:03baLLY[G, List @@ #] & /@ EdgeList[G]},\n  Graph[G, EdgeStyle -> Thread[EdgeList[G] -> (Directive[\:03baColour[#, 1.25], Thickness[0.012]] & /@ ks)],\n   VertexSize -> 0.15, VertexStyle -> GrayLevel[0.35], ImageSize -> 175,\n   PlotLabel -> Style[Row[{label, \"  min \", Min[ks]}], 9], ImagePadding -> {{6, 6}, {6, 30}}]];\nfam = {{\"tree\", tree}, {\"C\:2086\", CycleGraph[6]}, {\"Q\:2083\", HypercubeGraph[3]}, {\"K\:2085\", CompleteGraph[5]}, {\"Petersen\", PetersenGraph[]}, {\"grid 4\:00d74\", GridGraph[{4, 4}]}, {\"dumbbell\", dumbbell}};\nLegended[Grid[Partition[edgePlot[#[[2]], #[[1]]] & /@ fam, 4, 4, {1, 1}, \"\"]], \:03baLegend[1.25, Subscript[\"\:03ba\", \"LLY\"]]]", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Fig. 4. ", FontWeight -> "Bold", FontColor -> RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11]], "Seven graphs with every edge coloured by ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "LLY"]]], "."}], "Text"],
Cell["What to see: the grid\:2019s boundary edges are positive and its interior edges zero; the dumbbell\:2019s bridge path is the most negative part of the graph.", "Text"],
Cell["7. What a lower bound on \:03ba buys", "Section"],
Cell["7.1 Local to global", "Subsection"],
Cell["Ollivier proved that on a graph (more generally a geodesic space) it is enough to bound \:03ba on edges: if \:03ba(x, y) \:2265 k for every edge then \:03ba(x, y) \:2265 k for every pair, because W\:2081 is a metric and the triangle inequality adds up along a geodesic. On Q\:2083 with the \:00bd-lazy walk, every edge has \:03ba = 1/3; pairs at distance 2 and 3 have at least that.", "Text"],
Cell["With[{G = HypercubeGraph[3]}, {\:03ba[G, {1, 2}, 1/2], \:03ba[G, {1, 4}, 1/2], \:03ba[G, {1, 8}, 1/2], GraphDistance[G, 1, 4], GraphDistance[G, 1, 8]}]", "Input"],
Cell["7.2 Bonnet\:2013Myers: positive curvature bounds the diameter", "Subsection"],
Cell[TextData[{"On a Riemannian manifold Ric \:2265 (n \:2212 1)k > 0 forces diam \:2264 \:03c0/\:221ak. For graphs Lin, Lu and Yau proved: if ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "LLY"]]], " \:2265 k > 0 on every edge then diam(G) \:2264 2/k. The cube Q\:2083 attains it with equality: k = 2/3 and diam = 3."}], "Text"],
Cell["Table[With[{G = g[[2]], k = Min[\:03baLLY[g[[2]], List @@ #] & /@ EdgeList[g[[2]]]]}, {g[[1]], k, GraphDiameter[G], 2/k}],\n {g, {{\"K\:2085\", CompleteGraph[5]}, {\"Q\:2083\", HypercubeGraph[3]}, {\"star\", StarGraph[5]}, {\"K\:2083,\:2083\", CompleteGraph[{3, 3}]}}}]", "Input"],
Cell["7.3 Lichnerowicz: positive curvature opens a spectral gap", "Subsection"],
Cell[TextData[{"Ollivier proved that the first non-zero eigenvalue \:03bb\:2081 of the normalised Laplacian I \:2212 D\:207b\:00b9A is at least \:03ba\:2080 whenever \:03ba\:2080 \:2265 k on all edges (and Lin\:2013Lu\:2013Yau the same with ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "LLY"]]], "). The check lists min \:03ba\:2080, min ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "LLY"]]], " and \:03bb\:2081; the bound is informative only when the curvature is positive."}], "Text"],
Cell["Table[With[{G = c[[2]]}, {c[[1]], Min[\:03ba[G, List @@ #, 0] & /@ EdgeList[G]], Min[\:03baLLY[G, List @@ #] & /@ EdgeList[G]],\n   Round[Sort[Eigenvalues[N[IdentityMatrix[VertexCount[G]] - Inverse[DiagonalMatrix[VertexDegree[G]]] . AdjacencyMatrix[G]]]][[2]], 0.0001]}], {c, cases}]", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{"Check: K\:2085 has \:03bb\:2081 = 5/4 = ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "LLY"]]], " and Q\:2083 has \:03bb\:2081 = 2/3 = ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "LLY"]]], " (both equality); the star has \:03bb\:2081 = 1 \:2265 1/2; on the other graphs the curvature is \:2264 0 and the bound says nothing."}], "Text"],
Cell["8. Back on a surface: \:03ba \:2248 K\:03b5\:00b2/8", "Section"],
Cell["Let m\:2093 be the uniform measure on the geodesic \:03b5-ball around x in a surface of constant curvature K, and let y be at a small distance \:03b4 along a geodesic. Slide the ball along the geodesic (parallel transport). A point at signed distance \:03bb from the geodesic follows a curve at constant distance \:03bb, and the Jacobi equation J\:2033 + KJ = 0 gives its displacement \:03b4 cos(\:221aK \:03bb) + o(\:03b4). Averaging over the ball,", "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"W\:2081(m\:2093, ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["m", "y"]]], ") \:2264 \:03b4 \:00b7 E[cos(\:221aK \:03bb)] + o(\:03b4),   so   \:03ba(x, y) \:2265 1 \:2212 E[cos(\:221aK \:03bb)] = (K/2) E[\:03bb\:00b2] + O(\:03b5\:2074)."}], "Text"],
Cell["In the flat disc of radius \:03b5, E[\:03bb\:00b2] = \:03b5\:00b2/4 (\:03bb = \:03c1 sin \:03b1 in polar coordinates), so \:03ba \:2248 K\:03b5\:00b2/8. Ollivier showed the sliding coupling is optimal at this order, and in dimension N the same computation gives \:03ba = \:03b5\:00b2 Ric(v, v)/(2(N + 2)) + O(\:03b5\:00b3). The first check computes E[\:03bb\:00b2] exactly.", "Text"],
Cell["Integrate[(\:03c1 Sin[\:03b1])^2 \:03c1, {\:03b1, 0, 2 \:03c0}, {\:03c1, 0, \:03b5}]/(\:03c0 \:03b5^2)", "Input"],
Cell["Exact geometry of the ball: in polar coordinates (\:03c1, \:03b1) at x, spherical trigonometry gives sin(\:221aK \:03bb) = sin(\:221aK \:03c1) sin \:03b1 and the area element is sin(\:221aK \:03c1)/\:221aK d\:03c1 d\:03b1. The sliding-coupling curvature is then a plain integral. Its series in \:03b5, for any K:", "Text"],
Cell["kappaSliding[K_, n_] := Module[{e, \:03c1, \:03b1, sn = Sin[Sqrt[K] #]/Sqrt[K] &},\n  Normal@Series[Integrate[Normal@Series[(1 - Sqrt[1 - Sin[Sqrt[K] \:03c1]^2 Sin[\:03b1]^2]) sn[\:03c1], {\:03c1, 0, n + 1}], {\:03b1, 0, 2 \:03c0}, {\:03c1, 0, e}]/\n     (2 \:03c0 Integrate[Normal@Series[sn[\:03c1], {\:03c1, 0, n + 1}], {\:03c1, 0, e}]), {e, 0, n}] /. e -> \:03b5];\nkappaSliding[K, 6] // Expand", "Input"],
Cell["Check: K \:03b5\:00b2/8 \:2212 K\:00b2 \:03b5\:2074/64 + 11 K\:00b3 \:03b5\:2076/46080. The leading term is K/8 for the sphere (K = 1), 0 for the plane and \:22121/8 for the hyperbolic plane (K = \:22121). The \:03b5\:2074 and higher terms belong to this particular coupling: they bound \:03ba from below but are not claimed to be the exact W\:2081.", "Text"],
Cell["Dimension 3: on the unit 3-sphere (Ric = 2) the same sliding computation over a 3-ball gives \:03b5\:00b2 \:00b7 2/(2 \:00b7 5) = \:03b5\:00b2/5.", "Text"],
Cell["Module[{e, \:03c1, \:03c8}, Normal@Series[Integrate[Normal@Series[(1 - Sqrt[1 - Sin[\:03c1]^2 Sin[\:03c8]^2]) Sin[\:03c1]^2, {\:03c1, 0, 7}] Sin[\:03c8], {\:03c8, 0, \:03c0}, {\:03c1, 0, e}]/\n     (2 Integrate[Normal@Series[Sin[\:03c1]^2, {\:03c1, 0, 7}], {\:03c1, 0, e}]), {e, 0, 4}] /. e -> \:03b5]", "Input"],
Cell["Check: \:03b5\:00b2/5 \:2212 2\:03b5\:2074/75, whose leading term is Ollivier\:2019s \:03b5\:00b2 Ric/(2(N + 2)) with Ric = 2, N = 3.", "Text"],
Cell["Fig. 5 compares \:03ba/\:03b5\:00b2 from exact quadrature (written cancellation-free as s\:00b2/(1 + \:221a(1 \:2212 s\:00b2)) with s = sin(\:221aK \:03c1) sin \:03b1) with its limit K/8, for K = 1, 0, \:22121.", "Text"],
Cell["\:03baS2[\:03b5_?NumericQ] := NIntegrate[With[{s = Sin[\:03c1] Sin[\:03b1]}, s^2/(1 + Sqrt[1 - s^2])] Sin[\:03c1], {\:03c1, 0, \:03b5}, {\:03b1, 0, 2 \:03c0}]/(2 \:03c0 (1 - Cos[\:03b5]));\n\:03baH2[\:03b5_?NumericQ] := -NIntegrate[With[{s = Sinh[\:03c1] Sin[\:03b1]}, s^2/(1 + Sqrt[1 + s^2])] Sinh[\:03c1], {\:03c1, 0, \:03b5}, {\:03b1, 0, 2 \:03c0}]/(2 \:03c0 (Cosh[\:03b5] - 1));\nPlot[{\:03baS2[e]/e^2, 0, \:03baH2[e]/e^2, 1/8, -1/8}, {e, 0.02, 1.2}, PlotStyle -> {RGBColor[0.98, 0.56, 0.17], GrayLevel[0.4], RGBColor[0.18, 0.36, 0.78], Dashed, Dashed},\n PlotLegends -> {\"sphere K = 1\", \"plane K = 0\", \"hyperbolic K = \:22121\", \"K/8\"}, AxesLabel -> {\"\:03b5\", \"\:03ba/\:03b5\:00b2\"}, PlotRange -> {-0.2, 0.2},\n PlotLabel -> \"Ollivier curvature of \:03b5-balls tends to K/8\", ImageSize -> 480]", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Fig. 5. ", FontWeight -> "Bold", FontColor -> RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11]], "\:03ba/\:03b5\:00b2 against \:03b5 for the sphere, the plane and the hyperbolic plane, with the limits \:00b11/8."}], "Text"],
Cell["{\:03baS2[0.05]/0.05^2, \:03baH2[0.05]/0.05^2, N[kappaSliding[1, 6]/\:03b5^2 /. \:03b5 -> 0.3] - \:03baS2[0.3]/0.09}", "Input"],
Cell["Check: 0.12496 and \:22120.12504 at \:03b5 = 0.05; the series and the quadrature agree at \:03b5 = 0.3 to 3 \:00d7 10\:207b\:2078.", "Text"],
Cell["9. Exercises", "Section"],
Cell["1. Show by a transport plan and a test function that \:03ba\:2080 = 0 on every edge of the complete bipartite graph K\:2083,\:2083.", "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"2. Compute ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "1/2"]]], " on an edge of C\:2084 and of C\:2085 by hand. (Answers: 1/2 and 1/4.)"}], "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"3. A tree edge with endpoint degrees d\:2093 and ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["d", "y"]]], " has ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "LLY"]]], " = 2/d\:2093 + 2/", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["d", "y"]]], " \:2212 2. Check it on the star K\:2081,\:2084 and on the two kinds of edge of the binary tree."}], "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"4. Show that ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03ba", "LLY"]]], " = 2/n on every edge of the hypercube Q\:2099, so the diameter bound 2/\:03ba = n is attained for every n."}], "Text"],
Cell["5. Prove E[\:03bb\:00b2] = \:03b5\:00b2/4 for the uniform measure on a flat disc, and deduce the K/8.", "Text"],
Cell["Answers, computed:", "Text"],
Cell["{\:03ba[CompleteGraph[{3, 3}], {1, 4}, 0], {\:03ba[CycleGraph[4], {1, 2}, 1/2], \:03ba[CycleGraph[5], {1, 2}, 1/2]},\n {\:03baLLY[StarGraph[5], {1, 2}], 2/4 + 2/1 - 2, \:03baLLY[tree, {2, 4}], 2/3 + 2/3 - 2, \:03baLLY[tree, {4, 8}], 2/3 + 2/1 - 2},\n Table[{n, \:03baLLY[HypercubeGraph[n], {1, 2}], GraphDiameter[HypercubeGraph[n]]}, {n, 2, 5}]}", "Input"],
Cell["10. Summary", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"\:03ba(x, y) = 1 \:2212 W\:2081(m\:2093, ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["m", "y"]]], ")/d(x, y) needs only a distance and an averaging rule. On graphs it is computed by a transport linear program (or its 1-Lipschitz dual); laziness makes it linear in \:03b1 and yields the Lin\:2013Lu\:2013Yau curvature, itself a Laplacian formula. Positive lower bounds bound the diameter (Q\:2083: equality) and open a spectral gap (K\:2085, Q\:2083: equality). On a surface with \:03b5-balls it recovers the Gauss curvature: \:03ba = K\:03b5\:00b2/8 + O(\:03b5\:2074); in dimension N, \:03b5\:00b2 Ric/(2(N + 2))."}], "Text"],
Cell["\:00a9 2026 Computational Classnotes \:00b7 CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "Text"]
},
WindowTitle -> "Ollivier Curvature",
StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"
]
